"[2607.19012] Counterexamples to the xz-Conjecture and the Mathieu Conjecture for SU(2) (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19012)\nciteturn321view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19012\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 174\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Group Theory\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Jul 2026]\nL9: # Title:Counterexamples to the xz-Conjecture and the Mathieu Conjecture for SU(2)\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Christopher D. Long L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Counterexamples to the xz-Conjecture and the Mathieu Conjecture for SU(2), by Christopher D. Long\nL14: \nL15: cite6†View PDF cite7†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Let \\[\nL17: > {\\mathcal I}(h)=\\int_0^1\\int_{\\mathbb T}h(x,z)\\,\\frac{dz}{2\\pi iz}\\,dx\nL18: > \\qquad\nL19: > \\bigl(h\\in{\\mathbb C}[x,z,z^{-1}]\\bigr). \\] We give the three-term Laurent polynomial \\[\nL20: > f(x,z)=(1-z^{-1})\\bigl((1-x)+xz\\bigr) \\] for which \\[\nL21: > {\\mathcal I}(f^n)=0,\nL22: > \\qquad\nL23: > {\\mathcal I}(z^{-1}f^n)=\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n+1}\\neq0\nL24: > \\qquad(n\\geq1). \\] Since ${\\operatorname{Sp}}(f)=\\{-1,0,1\\}$, this disproves the $xz$-conjecture already with one interval variable and one torus variable, and it also shows that $\\ker{\\mathcal I}$ is not a Mathieu--Zhao subspace. Padding gives counterexamples to every mixed case of the $xz$-conjecture. Writing the coordinate functions on $SU(2)$ as \\[\nL25: > g=\\begin{pmatrix}a&c\\\\ b&d\\end{pmatrix}, \\] the same example lifts, through the integration formula of Müger and Tuset, to the regular functions \\[\nL26: > F=(1+c)(ad+b),\\qquad G=-c, \\] which satisfy \\[\nL27: > \\int_{SU(2)}F^n\\,dg=0,\nL28: > \\qquad\nL29: > \\int_{SU(2)}F^nG\\,dg=\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n+1}\\neq0 \\] for every $n\\geq1$. Thus the Mathieu conjecture for $SU(2)$ is false.\nL30: Comments:  | 8 pages, 0 figures\nL31: Subjects:  | Group Theory (math.GR); Commutative Algebra (math.AC); Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)\nL32: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | Primary 22E46, Secondary 43A05, 13A50, 14R15\nL33: Cite as:  | cite8†arXiv:2607.19012 [math.GR]\nL34:    | (or cite9†arXiv:2607.19012v1 [math.GR] for this version)\nL35:    | cite10†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2607.19012†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL36: ## Submission history\nL37: \nL38: From: Christopher Long [cite11†view email ]\nL39: [v1] Tue, 21 Jul 2026 11:54:10 UTC (7 KB)\nL40: \nL41: Full-text links:\nL42: \nL43: ## Access Paper:\nL44: \nL45: View a PDF of the paper titled Counterexamples to the xz-Conjecture and the Mathieu Conjecture for SU(2), by Christopher D. Long\nL46: \nL47:   * cite6†View PDF L48:   * cite7†HTML (experimental) L49:   * cite12†TeX Source L50: \nL51: cite13†view license L52: ### Current browse context:\nL53: \nL54: math.GR\nL55: \nL56: cite14†< prev |   cite15†next > L57: \nL58: cite16†new | cite17†recent | cite18†2026-07 L59: \nL60: Change to browse by:\nL61: \nL62: cite19†math L63: cite20†math.AC L64: cite21†math.CA L65: \nL66: ### References & Citations\nL67: \nL68:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L69:   * cite23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L70:   * cite24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L71: \nL72: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL73: \nL74: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL75: \nL76: [Button: ×]\nL77: \nL78: loading...\nL79: \nL80: Data provided by:\nL81: \nL82: ### Bookmark\nL83: \nL84: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL85: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL86: \nL87: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL88: \nL89: Bibliographic Explorer (cite25†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.or