1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 A counterexample to $xz(1,1)$ L18:   4. cite8†3 A family of circuit counterexamples L19:   5. cite9†4 The lift to $SU(2)$ L20:   6. cite10†5 The cancellation mechanism L21:   7. cite11†References L22: \nL23: cite12†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L24: \nL25: arXiv:2607.19012v1 [math.GR] 21 Jul 2026\nL26: # Counterexamples to the $xz$-Conjecture and the Mathieu Conjecture for $SU(2)$\nL27: \nL28: Christopher D. Long Email: galizur@gmail.com\nL29: \nL30: July 21, 2026\nL31: ###### Abstract\nL32: \nL33: Let\nL34: \nL35:  | $$\\mathcal{I}(h)=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{\\mathbb{T}}h(x,z)\\,\\frac{dz}{2\\pi iz}\\,dx\\qquad\\bigl(h\\in\\mathbb{C}[x,z,z^{-1}]\\bigr).$$  |\nL36: \nL37: We give the three-term Laurent polynomial\nL38: \nL39:  | $$f(x,z)=(1-z^{-1})\\bigl((1-x)+xz\\bigr)$$  |\nL40: \nL41: for which\nL42: \nL43:  | $$\\mathcal{I}(f^{n})=0,\\qquad\\mathcal{I}(z^{-1}f^{n})=\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n+1}\\neq 0\\qquad(n\\geq 1).$$  |\nL44: Since $\\operatorname{Sp}(f)=\\{-1,0,1\\}$, this disproves the $xz$-conjecture already with one interval variable and one torus variable, and it also shows that $\\ker\\mathcal{I}$ is not a Mathieu–Zhao subspace. Padding gives counterexamples to every mixed case of the $xz$-conjecture. Writing the coordinate functions on $SU(2)$ as\nL45: \nL46:  | $$g=\\begin{pmatrix}a&c\\\\\nL47: b&d\\end{pmatrix},$$  |\nL48: \nL49: the same example lifts, through the integration formula of Müger and Tuset, to the regular functions\nL50:  | $$F=(1+c)(ad+b),\\qquad G=-c,$$  |\nL51: \nL52: which satisfy\nL53: \nL54:  | $$\\int_{SU(2)}F^{n}\\,dg=0,\\qquad\\int_{SU(2)}F^{n}G\\,dg=\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n+1}\\neq 0$$  |\nL55: \nL56: for every $n\\geq 1$. Thus the Mathieu conjecture for $SU(2)$ is false.\nL57: \nL58: 2020 Mathematics Subject Classification. Primary 22E46; Secondary 43A05, 13A50, 14R15.\nL59: \nL60: Keywords. Mathieu conjecture, $SU(2)$, $xz$-conjecture, Laurent polynomial, Haar integral, Mathieu–Zhao subspace, Jacobian Conjecture.\nL61: ## 1 Introduction\nL62: \nL63: Let $K$ be a compact connected Lie group with normalized Haar measure $dg$. Mathieu conjectured that if $f$ and $g$ are complex-valued $K$-finite functions and\nL64: \nL65:  | $$\\int_{K}f(k)^{n}\\,dk=0\\qquad(n\\geq 1),$$  |\nL66: \nL67: then\nL68: \nL69:  | $$\\int_{K}f(k)^{n}g(k)\\,dk=0$$  |\nL70: for all sufficiently large $n$ [cite13†6 ]. Duistermaat and van der Kallen proved the conjecture for compact connected abelian groups [cite14†4 ]. Dings and Koelink studied the first nonabelian case $K=SU(2)$, proving the conjecture for several restricted classes and reducing the general problem to a support conjecture for matrix coefficients [cite15†2 ].\nL71: \nL72: Müger and Tuset subsequently gave an abelian reduction of the $SU(2)$ problem [cite16†7 ]. For integers $k,l\\geq 0$, an admissible function is a polynomial\nL73:  | $$h\\in\\mathbb{C}[x_{1},\\dots,x_{l},z_{1},z_{1}^{-1},\\dots,z_{k},z_{k}^{-1}],$$  |\nL74: \nL75: written as\nL76: \nL77:  | $$h(x,z)=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{k}}c_{m}(x)z^{m},\\qquad c_{m}\\in\\mathbb{C}[x_{1},\\dots,x_{l}].$$  |\nL78: \nL79: Its torus spectrum is\nL80: \nL81:  | $$\\operatorname{Sp}(h)=\\{m\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{k}:c_{m}\\neq 0\\}.$$  |\nL82: \nL83: The $xz$-conjecture asserts that\nL84: \n"
